      title: "Louis XIV and Absolutism",
      body: `## The Sun King

**Louis XIV** (1643–1715) came to the throne at the age of four and reigned for seventy-two years, longer than any other monarch in European history. During his childhood, France was governed by his mother, Anne of Austria, and her chief minister, the Italian-born **Cardinal Mazarin**. The Fronde left Louis with a lifelong fear of rebellion, a distrust of Paris, and a determination to rule personally. When Mazarin died in 1661, the twenty-two-year-old king announced that he would be his own chief minister.

Louis took the **sun** as his emblem: the source of light and life, around which everything revolves. He believed that he ruled by **divine right**, as God's lieutenant on earth, accountable to God alone. The bishop **Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet**, the court preacher and tutor to the king's son, set out the theory in *Politics Drawn from the Very Words of Holy Scripture*: "Royal authority is sacred. … God establishes kings as his ministers, and reigns through them over the peoples. … The royal throne is not the throne of a man, but the throne of God himself." The king's power was absolute, but not arbitrary: he was bound by the law of God and by the fundamental laws of the kingdom. The famous phrase "***L'État, c'est moi***"—"I am the state"—is almost certainly apocryphal, but it captures how many saw him. On his deathbed, he is reported to have said: "I am going, but the state will remain."

## Versailles

Louis's most famous creation was the palace of **Versailles**, built on the site of his father's hunting lodge, some twelve miles from Paris. Work began in the 1660s, and in **1682** the court moved there permanently. The palace, with its gardens laid out in geometric splendor by André Le Nôtre, its fountains, and its Hall of Mirrors, was the largest and most magnificent in Europe, and was imitated by kings and princes from Madrid to St. Petersburg.

Versailles was more than a palace. It was a machine for controlling the nobility. Louis required the great nobles to live at court, where they competed for his favor in an elaborate ritual of etiquette centered on his person. The king's rising in the morning (*lever*) and his going to bed at night (*coucher*) were public ceremonies, at which the highest nobles competed for the honor of handing him his shirt or holding his candle. A noble who was not seen at court was a noble without influence. The duke of **Saint-Simon**, who lived at Versailles and hated it, wrote in his memoirs that Louis "reduced everyone to subjection" by "distinctions and preferences," and that "no one was ever so skilled in the art of giving." Meanwhile, the real work of government was done by ministers drawn from the professional middle class, the "nobility of the robe," who owed everything to the king.

## Colbert

Louis's great finance minister, **Jean-Baptiste Colbert** (1619–1683), the son of a cloth merchant, worked to make France rich and powerful through **mercantilism**: the theory that a nation's wealth depended on accumulating gold and silver by exporting more than it imported. Colbert subsidized new industries, such as silk, glass, tapestries, and porcelain, regulated their quality, built roads and canals, including the great Canal du Midi linking the Atlantic and the Mediterranean, founded trading companies, built a powerful navy, and promoted colonies in North America, the Caribbean, and India. He also founded academies of science, painting, and architecture, to bring culture under royal patronage.

## Religion

Louis wanted one king, one law, one faith. In 1685, he revoked his grandfather Henry IV's **Edict of Nantes** and outlawed Protestantism in France. Protestant churches were destroyed, pastors exiled, and Protestant families forced to convert by quartering soldiers in their homes—the *dragonnades*. Some two hundred thousand Huguenots fled abroad, taking their skills and capital to the Netherlands, England, Prussia, and the American colonies. The revocation was popular among French Catholics, but it was an economic and diplomatic disaster.

## War

Above all, Louis sought **glory**, and glory meant war. For most of his reign, France was at war: against Spain, the Dutch Republic, the Holy Roman Empire, and England. His armies, the largest in Europe, conquered territory on France's northern and eastern frontiers, including Strasbourg and much of Flanders. But his ambitions united Europe against him. The great coalitions organized by the Dutch stadholder **William of Orange**, who became king of England in 1689, fought him to a standstill.

The last and greatest of his wars, the **War of the Spanish Succession** (1701–1714), began when the last Habsburg king of Spain died childless and left his throne to Louis's grandson. Britain, the Dutch, and Austria went to war to prevent the union of France and Spain. The English general **John Churchill, duke of Marlborough**, won a series of great victories, beginning at **Blenheim** in 1704. The **Treaty of Utrecht** (1713) allowed Louis's grandson to keep the Spanish throne as Philip V, on condition that the crowns of France and Spain would never be united. Britain gained Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland, Nova Scotia, and the right to supply enslaved Africans to the Spanish colonies.

## The legacy

When Louis XIV died in 1715, France was the most powerful and populous kingdom in Europe, with about twenty million people, and its language and culture dominated the continent. French was the language of diplomacy and of educated society from Lisbon to Moscow. But the kingdom was also exhausted by war and burdened by debt. The famine of 1693–1694 killed more than a million people, and that of 1709 another six hundred thousand. On his deathbed, Louis told his five-year-old great-grandson and heir: "I have loved war too much. Do not imitate me in that, nor in my excessive spending."

> **Pause and consider.** Louis XIV made the great nobles compete to hand him his shirt. Why might ritual and spectacle be effective tools of political power?`,
