      title: "England's Revolutions",
      body: `## King and Parliament

In England, the seventeenth century was dominated by a struggle between the crown and **Parliament**. The Stuart kings, **James I** (1603–1625) and his son **Charles I** (1625–1649), believed in the divine right of kings. James told Parliament that "kings are justly called gods, for they exercise a manner or resemblance of divine power upon earth." But English kings could not raise taxes without the consent of Parliament, which represented the landed gentry and the merchants, and which claimed ancient rights and privileges under the common law. Religion added fuel to the conflict: many members of Parliament were **Puritans**, who suspected Charles I and his archbishop, **William Laud**, of leading the Church of England back toward Rome.

In 1629, Charles dissolved Parliament and ruled without it for eleven years, raising money by dubious means. In 1637, he tried to impose an English-style prayer book on Presbyterian Scotland, provoking a Scottish rebellion. Needing money to fight the Scots, he was forced to summon Parliament in 1640. The **Long Parliament**, as it came to be called, demanded sweeping reforms and executed his chief minister. In 1642, war broke out between the king's supporters, the **Royalists** or "Cavaliers," and the supporters of Parliament, the "Roundheads."

## Civil war and regicide

Parliament's **New Model Army**, a disciplined, professional force of godly soldiers, many of them radical Puritans, commanded by **Oliver Cromwell**, a Puritan country gentleman of genius, defeated the king. Within the army, radical groups emerged: the **Levellers** demanded a written constitution, manhood suffrage, and religious toleration. In the famous **Putney Debates** of 1647, one Leveller officer, Thomas Rainsborough, declared: "I think that the poorest he that is in England hath a life to live, as the greatest he; and therefore … every man that is to live under a government ought first by his own consent to put himself under that government." The army's leaders rejected the Levellers' demands.

In January **1649**, after Charles had tried to restart the war, a purged Parliament tried him for treason against the people of England. Charles refused to recognize the court's authority, insisting that "a subject and a sovereign are clean different things." On January 30, 1649, he was beheaded outside the Banqueting House in Whitehall. Europe was shocked. England abolished the monarchy and the House of Lords and became a republic, the **Commonwealth**.

## Cromwell

The republic was dominated by Cromwell and the army. He crushed resistance in Ireland with notorious brutality, especially at the sieges of Drogheda and Wexford in 1649, and conquered Scotland. In 1653, he dissolved Parliament and became **Lord Protector**, ruling with powers greater than any king. He allowed considerable religious toleration among Protestants, and readmitted the Jews to England, from which they had been expelled in 1290. He refused the crown when it was offered to him. When he died in 1658, the regime collapsed.

## Restoration and the Glorious Revolution

In **1660**, Parliament invited Charles I's son to return as **Charles II**. The **Restoration** brought back the monarchy, the House of Lords, and the Church of England, and a reaction against Puritanism: the theaters reopened, and the court of Charles II was famous for its licentiousness. But Parliament retained its power over taxation, and the question of how far the crown's power extended remained unsettled.

It came to a head under Charles's brother, **James II** (1685–1688), who was openly Catholic. James appointed Catholics to high office in defiance of the law, maintained a large standing army, and claimed the right to suspend Parliament's laws. When his second wife gave birth to a son in June 1688, raising the prospect of a Catholic dynasty, a group of leading politicians invited James's Protestant daughter **Mary** and her Dutch husband, **William of Orange**, to intervene. William landed in England in November 1688 with a Dutch army. James's support collapsed, and he fled to France.

Parliament declared that James had abdicated, and offered the crown jointly to **William III and Mary II**, on condition that they accept the **Bill of Rights** (1689). The Bill of Rights declared that the king could not suspend laws, levy taxes, or maintain a standing army in peacetime without the consent of Parliament; that elections to Parliament should be free; that members should have freedom of speech in Parliament; that subjects had the right to petition the king; that Protestants might bear arms for their defense; and that excessive bail and "cruel and unusual punishments" were forbidden. The Toleration Act of 1689 granted freedom of worship to Protestant dissenters, though not to Catholics or Unitarians.

The English called this the **Glorious Revolution**, because it was largely bloodless in England (though not in Ireland and Scotland). It established that sovereignty in England lay with the "king in Parliament," and that the monarch ruled under the law. Over the next century, power shifted steadily from the crown to Parliament and to the ministers who commanded a majority there.

## Locke

The philosopher of the Glorious Revolution was **John Locke** (1632–1704), who had spent years in exile in the Netherlands. His ***Two Treatises of Government***, published in 1689 but written mostly in the early 1680s, offered a very different version of the social contract from Hobbes's. In Locke's state of nature, human beings are not at war; they are governed by a **law of nature**, discovered by reason, which teaches "that being all equal and independent, no one ought to harm another in his life, health, liberty, or possessions." Human beings have **natural rights** to life, liberty, and property, which they possess before any government exists.

People form governments by consent, Locke argued, to protect these rights more securely. Government is a trust, and its power is limited by its purpose. If a government violates the trust—if it tries to take people's property without their consent, or to reduce them to slavery under arbitrary power—it dissolves itself, and the people have the right to resist and to establish a new government.

Locke's ideas had an enormous influence, above all in America. Thomas Jefferson called Locke one of the three greatest men who ever lived (with Bacon and Newton), and the **Declaration of Independence**—with its "unalienable rights" to "life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness," its governments "deriving their just powers from the consent of the governed," and its right of the people "to alter or to abolish" a government that becomes destructive of these ends—is deeply Lockean. The American colonists, moreover, saw themselves as heirs to the English Bill of Rights; several of its provisions reappear, almost word for word, in the American Bill of Rights of 1791.

> **Pause and consider.** Hobbes and Locke both began with a state of nature and a social contract, but reached opposite conclusions about resistance. What was the crucial difference between them?`,
